WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.410
RAMESH SURAPARAJU:
SAP landscapes

00:00:01.410 --> 00:00:04.480
have advanced needs
for backup storage,

00:00:04.480 --> 00:00:07.830
especially when you design
high availability and disaster

00:00:07.830 --> 00:00:09.060
recovery.

00:00:09.060 --> 00:00:12.300
In Google Cloud, there are
many options for backup

00:00:12.300 --> 00:00:13.890
to choose from.

00:00:13.890 --> 00:00:15.790
What are these options?

00:00:15.790 --> 00:00:19.770
Which one is relevant for your
SAP deployment scenario or use

00:00:19.770 --> 00:00:20.950
case?

00:00:20.950 --> 00:00:23.690
What is unique to Google Cloud?

00:00:23.690 --> 00:00:26.290
Hi, I am Ramesh Suraparaju.

00:00:26.290 --> 00:00:29.130
And in this video, I will
answer these questions

00:00:29.130 --> 00:00:33.180
on backup strategies and
solutions for SAP systems

00:00:33.180 --> 00:00:34.110
on Google Cloud.

00:00:40.160 --> 00:00:43.610
Customers run mission-critical
business processes

00:00:43.610 --> 00:00:46.220
on their SAP systems.

00:00:46.220 --> 00:00:49.040
Downtimes must be
kept to a minimum

00:00:49.040 --> 00:00:51.800
to ensure business continuity.

00:00:51.800 --> 00:00:55.880
Regular backup of data
ensures speedy recovery

00:00:55.880 --> 00:00:58.910
from potential data loss.

00:00:58.910 --> 00:01:01.400
Google Cloud offers
cost-effective native

00:01:01.400 --> 00:01:08.180
capabilities as well as partner
solutions for backup of data.

00:01:08.180 --> 00:01:11.120
Before we look at
various backup options,

00:01:11.120 --> 00:01:14.850
let us look at a
typical SAP deployment.

00:01:14.850 --> 00:01:20.870
Here is the architecture of
SAP deployment on Google Cloud.

00:01:20.870 --> 00:01:25.220
The application layer is a set
of one or more SAP NetWeaver

00:01:25.220 --> 00:01:29.660
application servers deployed on
Compute Engine with associated

00:01:29.660 --> 00:01:33.470
persistent disk storage in a
specific zone within a Google

00:01:33.470 --> 00:01:35.200
Cloud region.

00:01:35.200 --> 00:01:39.520
The database layer has a
single database server for SAP

00:01:39.520 --> 00:01:42.790
and is also deployed on
Compute Engine with associated

00:01:42.790 --> 00:01:45.700
persistent disk storage.

00:01:45.700 --> 00:01:49.210
Zooming in on the storage
part of this architecture,

00:01:49.210 --> 00:01:53.350
we can see that there are a few
specific volumes at file system

00:01:53.350 --> 00:01:56.840
level that are
relevant for backup.

00:01:56.840 --> 00:02:00.440
In the application layer, these
are the virtual machine boot

00:02:00.440 --> 00:02:06.490
disk, the folders for
SAP MNT, and swap space.

00:02:06.490 --> 00:02:09.880
At the database layer, the
relevant volumes are, again,

00:02:09.880 --> 00:02:13.270
the boot disk, folders
that contain the data

00:02:13.270 --> 00:02:17.530
and logs of the database, and
folders that hold the database

00:02:17.530 --> 00:02:19.910
backups.

00:02:19.910 --> 00:02:22.730
Google Cloud
recommends to separate

00:02:22.730 --> 00:02:26.270
these backup relevant
folders from the boot disks

00:02:26.270 --> 00:02:29.630
into separate persistent disks.

00:02:29.630 --> 00:02:32.870
Google also recommends
that assisted disks

00:02:32.870 --> 00:02:37.250
are used, especially for
SAP HANA installations,

00:02:37.250 --> 00:02:41.420
due to their performance
and throughput requirements.

00:02:41.420 --> 00:02:45.650
HDD can be used for cost
optimization and backup

00:02:45.650 --> 00:02:47.130
purposes.

00:02:47.130 --> 00:02:50.340
Now let us look at an example
of a centralized S/4HANA

00:02:50.340 --> 00:02:52.640
deployment on Google Cloud.

00:02:52.640 --> 00:02:56.300
When I select this deployment
in the Google Cloud console

00:02:56.300 --> 00:02:58.800
and click on name
of the instance,

00:02:58.800 --> 00:03:02.860
we can see the disk layout
for this deployment.

00:03:02.860 --> 00:03:06.880
Here you can see the boot
disk of the S4 instance

00:03:06.880 --> 00:03:10.660
and all the other
backup-relevant volumes created

00:03:10.660 --> 00:03:15.110
and attached to the S4 instance.

00:03:15.110 --> 00:03:18.440
Now let me introduce you
to the backup options

00:03:18.440 --> 00:03:23.350
for each component of a SAP
installation on Google Cloud.

00:03:23.350 --> 00:03:26.410
Persistent disk snapshots
are global resources

00:03:26.410 --> 00:03:31.090
that offer a simple and
straightforward way for backup.

00:03:31.090 --> 00:03:36.040
Cloud storage buckets offer
regional, dual-region, and

00:03:36.040 --> 00:03:38.950
multi-region storage
options that can support

00:03:38.950 --> 00:03:43.360
high availability and disaster
recovery architectures of SAP

00:03:43.360 --> 00:03:45.280
installations.

00:03:45.280 --> 00:03:49.240
Cloud storage also offers
different storage clusters,

00:03:49.240 --> 00:03:53.530
like nearline, coldline,
and archive storage,

00:03:53.530 --> 00:03:55.840
with rule-based
configuration for time

00:03:55.840 --> 00:04:01.390
to live and transitioning
between these storage classes.

00:04:01.390 --> 00:04:04.900
Partner solutions for shared
file systems like NetApp,

00:04:04.900 --> 00:04:08.620
Cloud volumes also offer
a resilient backup option

00:04:08.620 --> 00:04:11.770
using Cloud storage buckets.

00:04:11.770 --> 00:04:15.580
Cloud Filestore is a
cost-effective option

00:04:15.580 --> 00:04:18.370
for single-zone
SAP installations,

00:04:18.370 --> 00:04:22.210
like SAP HANA scale-out,
in a single zone

00:04:22.210 --> 00:04:25.960
where high availability is
achieved by standby nodes

00:04:25.960 --> 00:04:27.640
within the same zone.

00:04:27.640 --> 00:04:32.290
How can these options be
used to back up SAP systems?

00:04:32.290 --> 00:04:36.640
At the application layer,
you can schedule PD snapshots

00:04:36.640 --> 00:04:42.430
as backup for disks of the SAP
NetWeaver application server.

00:04:42.430 --> 00:04:46.040
For multi-instance installations
where a shared file

00:04:46.040 --> 00:04:48.580
storage like NetApp
is used, you can

00:04:48.580 --> 00:04:51.310
use the features of the
shared file solution

00:04:51.310 --> 00:04:55.480
to back up disks to
Cloud storage buckets.

00:04:55.480 --> 00:05:01.670
At the database layer 2, you
can schedule snapshots of PDs.

00:05:01.670 --> 00:05:04.280
In case of SAP
HANA database, you

00:05:04.280 --> 00:05:08.180
can bypass the persistent
disks for HANA backup volumes

00:05:08.180 --> 00:05:11.090
and use Backint agent
for Google Cloud

00:05:11.090 --> 00:05:14.360
to directly back up to
Cloud storage buckets.

00:05:14.360 --> 00:05:17.850
Let me drill down into
the application layer.

00:05:17.850 --> 00:05:21.380
This architecture shows a
multi-instance SAP NetWeaver

00:05:21.380 --> 00:05:25.790
installation with separate
VMs for primary and additional

00:05:25.790 --> 00:05:29.930
application servers, as well
as the central services.

00:05:29.930 --> 00:05:32.900
You can see that these
snapshots are created

00:05:32.900 --> 00:05:35.750
for each of the
backup-relevant disks,

00:05:35.750 --> 00:05:41.840
like the boot disk, SAP MNT,
and the swap space folders.

00:05:41.840 --> 00:05:45.410
You can schedule these
snapshots for automating

00:05:45.410 --> 00:05:51.010
the backup mechanism
across your SAP landscape.

00:05:51.010 --> 00:05:53.980
Now let me show you how
to schedule a snapshot

00:05:53.980 --> 00:05:56.480
of a persistent disk.

00:05:56.480 --> 00:06:00.470
Here you can see the
existing snapshot schedules

00:06:00.470 --> 00:06:06.140
for a centralized two-tier
deployment of a S/4HANA system,

00:06:06.140 --> 00:06:10.220
where the database and
application server are all

00:06:10.220 --> 00:06:13.160
installed on a single server.

00:06:13.160 --> 00:06:17.240
While you can see schedules
exist for boot, swap,

00:06:17.240 --> 00:06:20.110
and other disks of
this deployment,

00:06:20.110 --> 00:06:25.490
a schedule for backup of
HANA data is still missing.

00:06:25.490 --> 00:06:28.760
Let us go ahead and
see how to create one.

00:06:28.760 --> 00:06:32.870
Here, I navigate to the
relevant persistent disk, which

00:06:32.870 --> 00:06:38.390
is called s4hana-data, and
see that there is no snapshot

00:06:38.390 --> 00:06:40.850
scheduled for this disk.

00:06:40.850 --> 00:06:45.520
I will proceed to edit the disk
configuration to create one.

00:06:45.520 --> 00:06:51.460
Notice that you need not
stop the VM to do this.

00:06:51.460 --> 00:06:54.340
Once I am in the Edit
mode of the disk,

00:06:54.340 --> 00:06:57.070
I can choose to create
a snapshot schedule

00:06:57.070 --> 00:07:01.090
and specify required
configuration like name,

00:07:01.090 --> 00:07:04.330
description, location
of the snapshot,

00:07:04.330 --> 00:07:08.290
regional or multiregional,
the scheduled frequency,

00:07:08.290 --> 00:07:11.470
and start time for
taking the snapshot.

00:07:11.470 --> 00:07:15.010
I can also specify
the deletion rules.

00:07:15.010 --> 00:07:19.330
In my example, I want
it to be 90 days.

00:07:19.330 --> 00:07:22.630
After entering all
parameters, I click Create.

00:07:25.160 --> 00:07:28.900
Finally, I save the
disk configuration.

00:07:28.900 --> 00:07:33.010
The schedule to take snapshots
for the HANA data disk

00:07:33.010 --> 00:07:35.500
is now active.

00:07:35.500 --> 00:07:38.500
You can check, in the
snapshot schedules page,

00:07:38.500 --> 00:07:42.580
that there is now a new schedule
to back up the HANA data

00:07:42.580 --> 00:07:43.840
volume.

00:07:43.840 --> 00:07:46.720
Productive SAP
installations often

00:07:46.720 --> 00:07:50.290
use shared file storage
in the application layer

00:07:50.290 --> 00:07:54.750
to enable faster failover
of application servers.

00:07:54.750 --> 00:07:58.350
Shared file solutions,
like NetApp cloud volumes,

00:07:58.350 --> 00:08:01.230
also offer configuration
of snapshots

00:08:01.230 --> 00:08:04.950
of the shared disks to
Google Cloud Storage buckets.

00:08:04.950 --> 00:08:07.820
Let me move on to
the database layer.

00:08:07.820 --> 00:08:10.820
If the database
server is SAP HANA,

00:08:10.820 --> 00:08:13.850
you have two alternatives.

00:08:13.850 --> 00:08:16.390
The first option is
to create schedules

00:08:16.390 --> 00:08:20.620
to snapshot the relevant disks
and automate a regular backup

00:08:20.620 --> 00:08:22.580
of your database.

00:08:22.580 --> 00:08:26.990
Alternative option is to
use the SAP HANA Backint

00:08:26.990 --> 00:08:29.680
agent for Google Cloud.

00:08:29.680 --> 00:08:34.159
The HANA Backint agent is a
free-of-cost software agent

00:08:34.159 --> 00:08:36.559
that can be downloaded
from Google Cloud

00:08:36.559 --> 00:08:42.049
to be installed on the VM
that runs your SAP HANA.

00:08:42.049 --> 00:08:44.750
Once the Backint
agent is installed,

00:08:44.750 --> 00:08:47.450
you can specify the
Cloud storage bucket

00:08:47.450 --> 00:08:51.780
into which the backup
files are to be stored.

00:08:51.780 --> 00:08:56.720
You can then turn on the options
for catalog and log backup

00:08:56.720 --> 00:08:59.660
to use the Backint agent.

00:08:59.660 --> 00:09:03.830
Another feature is to enable
parallel backup streams

00:09:03.830 --> 00:09:07.790
to decrease backup
times drastically.

00:09:07.790 --> 00:09:12.350
When this configuration is
done, your SAP HANA system

00:09:12.350 --> 00:09:16.520
and databases will now
use the Backint agent

00:09:16.520 --> 00:09:23.070
to back up directly to
Google Cloud Storage bucket.

00:09:23.070 --> 00:09:27.090
All the subsequent backups that
are triggered by your SAP HANA

00:09:27.090 --> 00:09:31.410
database are directly stored
in the specified Google Cloud

00:09:31.410 --> 00:09:33.960
Storage bucket instead
of a file system

00:09:33.960 --> 00:09:37.020
folder of a persistent disk.

00:09:37.020 --> 00:09:39.810
In this way, you
can bypass the need

00:09:39.810 --> 00:09:43.525
to provision a persistent
disk for HANA backup volumes.

00:09:46.050 --> 00:09:49.830
Here, you can see the storage
bucket in which the HANA backup

00:09:49.830 --> 00:09:51.670
files are stored.

00:09:51.670 --> 00:09:56.070
You can see that the systems
and the tenant databases

00:09:56.070 --> 00:09:58.230
have separate backup folders.

00:09:58.230 --> 00:10:01.650
And of course, you can
restore a database directly

00:10:01.650 --> 00:10:05.310
from its bucket location.

00:10:05.310 --> 00:10:08.640
To complete the database
layer, this architecture

00:10:08.640 --> 00:10:13.710
shows the snapshots scheduled
for all backup-relevant folders

00:10:13.710 --> 00:10:16.200
in case of any other
database server,

00:10:16.200 --> 00:10:20.100
like Sybase ASE
or MS SQL server.

00:10:20.100 --> 00:10:24.120
You can refer to the relevant
database server documentation

00:10:24.120 --> 00:10:28.530
for the exact location and
names of the data, log,

00:10:28.530 --> 00:10:30.300
and backup folders.

00:10:30.300 --> 00:10:34.230
To summarize, Google Cloud
recommends separate disks

00:10:34.230 --> 00:10:38.700
for backup-relevant volumes
of your SAP deployment.

00:10:38.700 --> 00:10:41.670
You can schedule snapshots
of persistent disks

00:10:41.670 --> 00:10:46.320
as primary mechanism for
backup of SAP systems.

00:10:46.320 --> 00:10:49.590
Shared file solutions
also support snapshots

00:10:49.590 --> 00:10:52.470
to Google Cloud Storage.

00:10:52.470 --> 00:10:56.220
You can use SAP HANA Backint
agent for Google Cloud

00:10:56.220 --> 00:10:59.700
to bypass persistent disks
for HANA backup volumes.

00:11:02.700 --> 00:11:04.800
You can find a lot
more information

00:11:04.800 --> 00:11:08.970
about backup options and
solutions for SAP deployments

00:11:08.970 --> 00:11:13.470
on Google Cloud at the
links below this video.

00:11:13.470 --> 00:11:19.350
Also, please navigate
to cloud.google.com/sap

00:11:19.350 --> 00:11:23.710
for information about other
topics related to SAP on Google

00:11:23.710 --> 00:11:25.290
Cloud.

00:11:25.290 --> 00:11:26.430
Thanks for listening.

00:11:26.430 --> 00:11:30.080
[MUSIC PLAYING]

